WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
Let’s talk about a new law in France that they want to bring in.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.800
It’s called Reinforcing Republican Principles.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.640
The French president says it's about preventing religious extremism.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.720
The bill doesn’t actually single out any one religion.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.360
But some French Muslims say it’s targeting them and that it’s being used to score political points.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.360
So what’s in this proposed law?

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.960
Is it as bad as some people say?

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.720
And what does it have to do with France’s whole thing about secularism?

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.080
When it comes to religions France is a pretty diverse place.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:54.120
Just over half of its population identifies as Christian, it has the largest Muslim and Jewish communities in Europe 

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.640
and a third of people in France don’t affiliate with any faith at all.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:06.280
And all of those communities have been able to coexist in part because of a legal principle called laicite, which roughly translates as secularism.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:14.160
It was passed into law in 1905 to separate the French republic from the Catholic Church after years of power struggles.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:20.600
These days you can see laicite in action in the way the French president, for example, isn’t sworn in using a holy book.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.320
But over the years French governments have interpreted laicite in new ways.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:54.200
They’ve brought in all kinds of measures to keep religion out of public life to the point though that some people accuse the state of going after religion.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.720
Like a law in 2004 that banned religious symbols in public schools.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.200
So what does laicite, or secularism, have to do with this new bill?

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.760
Well the proposed law doesn’t explicitly mention laicite. 

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:40.600
But it talks about republican principles like free thinking and free speech, which President Emmanuel Macron says are under threat because of terrorism.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:49.960
Since 2015 more than 200 people in France have been killed — mostly by attackers recruited or inspired by ISIL or al-Qaeda.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.800
Like the attack on staff at the satirical magazine Charlie Hebdo.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.960
It had published caricatures of the Prophet Muhammad, whose depiction is widely forbidden in Islam.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.680
90 people killed at the Bataclan theatre. 

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.600
A truck driven through crowds.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.440
A Christmas market shooting.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.600
And then there was everything that happened in 2020.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:23.760
Charlie Hebdo republished the prophet’s caricature and two people were stabbed outside the magazine’s old building.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.760
That’s when Macron announced he was drafting this bill. 

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:34.000
But that didn’t go down well with leaders in Muslim-majority countries. 

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.440
Protests spread and some people boycotted French products.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:05.400
Not long afterwards a teacher in France showed students the caricature during a lesson about free speech. 

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.880
There was a social media campaign against him, he was tracked down and beheaded. 

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.560
And then a week later several people were killed inside a church.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.240
So Macron went ahead and proposed his bill. 

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.120
He gave the problem a name — Islamist separatism — and explained it this way.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.960
OK, so what’s in this proposed law?

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.480
Things like tougher penalties for people who threaten or attack public workers in the name of religion.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.240
More powers to go after hate speech online.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.000
And limits on big foreign donations to religious institutions.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:55.240
But there’s other stuff in there like giving officials more power to stop forced marriages, polygamy and anyone issuing “virginity certificates” for women about to marry.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.960
There are even restrictions on religious private schools and home-schooling.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.480
That’s the thing about this bill. It seems to be about a lot of things.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:47.480
Laicite, terrorism, mosques, schools, women — even what the politicians describe as “separatism”.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:52.240
Some take that to mean everything from radicalised young people to communities not assimilating.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.240
Even as far as how halal and kosher food are stocked separately.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.080
And it’s not just this bill. There’s a lot more going on.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:22.600
The government has closed down several mosques and Muslim associations for having radical links, which they’ve denied.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:29.480
They’ve also got Muslim religious leaders to agree on a charter of principles that rejects political Islam and foreign interference. 

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.360
So how do Muslims in France feel about all of this?

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.240
Well, religious leaders are split. 

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:55.000
One Muslim association said “separatism” is “a reality that must be faced”.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:02.680
At the same time a lot of people feel this bill just reinforces a feeling that French Muslims are never French enough.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:23.240
But if you listen to French commentators there’s another theory about why Macron’s pushing this bill.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:31.400
It’s that ahead of elections in 2022 he’s making a political calculation to win support on the far right and on the left.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:55.240
France’s parliament and senate still have to vote on the bill before it becomes law.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:08:04.160
They’ll have to weigh up a lot of things but chief among them is whether this proposed law goes against the very principles people say it’s trying to defend.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.480
We love hearing about what you’d like us to explain next. 

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.600
We’re actually working on some of your ideas now. 

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:18.280
So if you come across any news stories you find complicated let us know in the comments wherever you’re watching this video. 

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:19.680
And I’ll see you next week. 

